# Large local-only directories — never upload to Vercel
# Use /prefix to match root-only (avoid excluding apps/api/dist/deck/data/)
/cache
/assets
/data
/image-cache
/extract
/deploy
/supabase
/research
*.dump
*.tar
*.log
*.jsonl
*.webp
*.avif
# ...except the committed landing-page art. Without these negations the two rules
# above drop every optimised marketing asset from the upload, and the deployed
# hero/accents 404 into their CSS gradient fallbacks with no build-time error.
# Mirrors the same negation pair in .gitignore.
!apps/web/public/marketing/*.webp
!apps/web/public/marketing/*.avif
# ...and Deck-E's card back, which hit exactly the failure the comment above
# predicts. `.gitignore` swallowed it first, so it 404'd in production; adding
# the negation THERE was not enough, because this file drops it from the upload
# as well and the two are independent. The build gate in
# apps/web/scripts/check-precache.mjs is what caught the second half — it failed
# the Vercel deploy while the identical tree built cleanly from a fresh clone,
# which is the difference between "in git" and "uploaded".
!apps/web/public/models/decke/*.webp
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.owner-temp-password

# Deck-E comparison artifacts for /dev/decke-compare. Gitignored, so a CI build
# never sees them — but `vercel --prod` from a maintainer machine copies
# public/ wholesale and would ship 3.3 MB of them.
apps/web/public/models/decke/decke.orig.glb
apps/web/public/models/decke/decke.min.glb

# The embed model NEVER rides up from a local tree - every build takes
# scripts/fetch-embed-model.mjs's verified-download path (round 12b: a CLI
# deploy carried the local file and silently bypassed the sha256 check).
apps/api/assets/embed/*.onnx
